WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.200
This is one of the most&nbsp;
famous paintings in the world.

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.480
Millions of people contemplate&nbsp;
it every year at the Vatican,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.760
and thousands of books have analyzed it. For&nbsp;
more than five hundred years, historians have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.760
studied every single face that appears in it&nbsp;
in order to identify each figure in this scene.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:22.400
However, there is a small object,&nbsp;
almost at the center of the composition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:25.920
that has gone practically unnoticed all this time.&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.360
A simple inkwell resting on the corner of&nbsp;
a marble block, practically about to fall.

00:00:31.360 --> 00:00:35.360
I would dare to say that very few people&nbsp;
in the world have tried to interpret&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:40.160
this object. However, according to&nbsp;
some historians, that small object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.880
could be the key to understanding the&nbsp;
complete meaning of this masterpiece.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:50.560
The School of Athens was painted&nbsp;
by Raphael between 1509 and 1511,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:55.600
and as I told you before, it is&nbsp;
located in the Vatican Museums in Rome.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.640
But to understand its importance, we&nbsp;
need to know the context of its creation.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:07.680
In 1509, Raphael was barely twenty-six years&nbsp;
old when Pope Julius II called him to redecorate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.000
a set of rooms in the Apostolic Palace of the&nbsp;
Vatican. At that moment, it was the most important&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:19.600
commission of his life. And the pressure could&nbsp;
not have been greater: just a few steps away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.480
Michelangelo was painting the ceiling of&nbsp;
the Sistine Chapel at that very moment.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:30.240
The first room Raphael painted was the Stanza&nbsp;
della Segnatura, the Room of the Signature.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.680
In that room, the most important documents of&nbsp;
the Church were signed and sealed. And it also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.680 --> 00:01:41.440
functioned as the papal library and as the seat&nbsp;
of the most powerful tribunal in Catholicism.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:46.000
Raphael dedicated each wall to one&nbsp;
of the great themes of the library:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.760
theology, poetry, law, and philosophy.

00:01:49.760 --> 00:01:55.760
And for the wall of philosophy, he created&nbsp;
this scene. An imaginary, timeless space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.760 --> 00:02:01.040
where he gathered the most important thinkers&nbsp;
of a thousand years of history. In it we see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:07.200
Plato and Aristotle walking through the center.&nbsp;
Pythagoras writing in the foreground. Euclid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:13.600
tracing figures with a compass, and Diogenes lying&nbsp;
on the steps without a single sign of concern.

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.920
An impossible gathering of the&nbsp;
most brilliant minds of antiquity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:23.920
painted with a perfection that turned this fresco&nbsp;
into the definitive symbol of the Renaissance.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:30.160
But behind that perfection, Raphael faced a&nbsp;
problem that almost ruined the entire project.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.080
Let us think for a moment about the&nbsp;
technical challenge of this work.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:38.480
To give you a comparison, Michelangelo,&nbsp;
in the Sistine Chapel, was painting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:44.480
biblical heroes. His mission was to paint&nbsp;
figures with visually unmistakable stories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:50.720
like the Flood or the Creation of Adam. Raphael,&nbsp;
on the other hand, had to paint philosophers.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:56.240
As is obvious, philosophers have no visual&nbsp;
attributes. They perform no miracles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.080
they have no battles behind them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:03.200
much less famous scenes. They are men with&nbsp;
robes and beards who dedicate themselves to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:09.440
thinking. So how could we tell Antisthenes&nbsp;
from Xenophon? Or Diogenes from Socrates?

00:03:09.440 --> 00:03:12.720
According to the analysis&nbsp;
of historian Kelly Grovier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:18.640
when Raphael began the preparatory sketches,&nbsp;
this problem became increasingly evident.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:22.480
Raphael found that his work was&nbsp;
turning into a crowd of ambiguous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.680
figures floating in a sea of anonymous&nbsp;
thought that would serve no purpose.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:32.960
So Raphael made a brilliant decision.&nbsp;
Instead of fighting against this confusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:39.600
he embraced it. And he turned the ambiguity of&nbsp;
the identities into the very message of the work.&nbsp;

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.560
And the proof is in the figures themselves.

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:49.520
Let us look at Plato, at the center of the fresco.&nbsp;
His aged face and his beard are not invented. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.760
are the portrait of Leonardo da Vinci, exactly as&nbsp;
he appears in his famous self-portrait. And his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:02.320
hand pointing toward the sky is the exact same&nbsp;
gesture Leonardo painted in his Last Supper.

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.040
So in a single figure, Raphael&nbsp;
painted three distinct identities:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:11.200
one of them the Greek philosopher,&nbsp;
then the genius of the Renaissance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.120
and finally, the gesture that&nbsp;
points toward the transcendent.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:20.160
And that same pattern repeats throughout&nbsp;
the fresco. The Pythagoras writing in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:25.040
foreground has also been identified as&nbsp;
a double portrait of Saint Matthew with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:31.280
his angel. The geometer with the compass is&nbsp;
Archimedes and Euclid at the same time. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.280 --> 00:04:37.120
astronomer holding the sphere of stars merges&nbsp;
Strabo and Zoroaster into a single image.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:42.560
So nothing in this painting has a single&nbsp;
identity. Everything flows within it.

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:45.680
But the question you are&nbsp;
probably asking yourself is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:50.240
how do we know this was intentional&nbsp;
and not a simple oversight? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.240 --> 00:04:55.120
this is where we arrive at the inkwell I&nbsp;
mentioned at the beginning of this video.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:59.680
But before we continue, I would like to remind&nbsp;
you that if you would like to have The School&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:04.000
of Athens in your collection, it&nbsp;
is now available in our catalog.

