00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.160
Now then, let us continue.

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:40.880
To understand the inkwell, we first need&nbsp;
to talk about the figure it belongs to.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.120
At the center of the foreground, sitting&nbsp;
alone, leaning on a marble block,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.760
there is a pensive man writing. His pen&nbsp;
is paused in the middle of a thought,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.720
and his expression is commonly described&nbsp;
as melancholic, almost dejected.

00:05:54.720 --> 00:05:59.200
Something quite curious about this figure is&nbsp;
that it does not appear in the preparatory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:04.560
sketches of the fresco. Raphael must have&nbsp;
added it at the last moment, when the work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:10.080
was practically finished. And historians have&nbsp;
a fascinating theory that could explain this.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.200
For centuries, this figure has been&nbsp;
recognized as a tribute to Michelangelo,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:20.160
as it unmistakably shares the artist's&nbsp;
facial features. It is believed that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:24.400
Raphael, after secretly seeing the&nbsp;
progress of the Sistine Chapel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:29.360
was so impressed that he decided to&nbsp;
include his great rival in the fresco.

00:06:29.360 --> 00:06:34.160
But as you might expect, this figure&nbsp;
is not only Michelangelo. It is also,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.160
at the same time, the Greek&nbsp;
philosopher Heraclitus.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:42.320
And that double identity is key&nbsp;
to what I am about to tell you.

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:48.400
Heraclitus is the philosopher known for a&nbsp;
single idea, but one that is, at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.320
one of the most famous ideas in all of ancient&nbsp;
philosophy. The idea that no one can step into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.320 --> 00:07:01.520
the same river twice. For Heraclitus, everything&nbsp;
flows, everything changes, and nothing remains.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:07.760
But although this idea has endured to this day,&nbsp;
history was cruelly ironic with him. Of all his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:15.280
writings, absolutely nothing survived. Today we&nbsp;
do not have a single complete work by Heraclitus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:21.040
So the philosopher who said that nothing remains&nbsp;
ended up becoming the proof of his own theory.

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:24.720
And this is precisely where the&nbsp;
inkwell starts to make sense.

00:07:24.720 --> 00:07:29.680
If we go back to the painting, we find that&nbsp;
small object next to Michelangelo's arm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:33.680
teetering on the corner of the marble&nbsp;
block, one step away from falling and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.680 --> 00:07:39.520
spilling. According to historians, Raphael&nbsp;
painted the exact moment when Heraclitus's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:46.160
ink was still fresh. In other words, the instant&nbsp;
just before his words were lost forever in time.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:51.600
But this symbol becomes even more interesting&nbsp;
when we remember the place where it was painted.

00:07:53.120 --> 00:07:57.175
This fresco is located in the room known as&nbsp;
the Room of the Signature. And it receives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.175 --> 00:08:00.560
that name because it was the place where the&nbsp;
Pope signed the most important decrees of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:06.240
Church. In other words, the place where ink&nbsp;
became eternal and unquestionable doctrine.

00:08:06.240 --> 00:08:09.280
And at the center of the fresco&nbsp;
presiding over that room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:13.680
Raphael painted an inkwell about to&nbsp;
fall. The inkwell of a philosopher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.480
who said that nothing remains, and whose&nbsp;
own words disappeared without a trace.

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:23.600
It is hard to imagine a more subversive&nbsp;
message in a place like that. While down below,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:28.560
documents destined to last forever were&nbsp;
being signed, up above, a small object&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:35.600
hinted that no ink is eternal. That no identity&nbsp;
is. And of course, that no authority is either.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:39.280
And this is where all the pieces&nbsp;
of the fresco fall into place.

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:46.160
If we recall the fluid identities, Plato is also&nbsp;
Leonardo. Pythagoras is also Saint Matthew. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:52.640
the geometer is Archimedes and Euclid at the same&nbsp;
time. Without the inkwell, all of these fusions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:58.160
would seem like simple mistakes. The oversights&nbsp;
of a painter who could not define his characters.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:01.360
But with Heraclitus's inkwell at the center,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.360 --> 00:09:07.200
everything makes sense. The identities in this&nbsp;
fresco flow because that is exactly the thesis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:13.520
of the work. Raphael did not paint a catalog of&nbsp;
philosophers as we had believed for centuries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:18.320
but rather painted Heraclitus's&nbsp;
philosophy in action. A river of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.320 --> 00:09:24.800
identities where no one is a single thing, and&nbsp;
where everything mixes and transforms constantly.

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:30.320
In fact, some historians believe that if we&nbsp;
removed that small object from the fresco,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:34.800
the entire work would dissolve into&nbsp;
a chaos of confusing figures. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.800 --> 00:09:38.640
the inkwell is the axis that&nbsp;
holds the whole system together.

00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:44.080
But perhaps the most revealing detail is that&nbsp;
the figure of Heraclitus does not appear in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:50.080
preparatory sketches. Raphael added it at the&nbsp;
end, when the fresco was practically finished.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.080 --> 00:09:54.960
I would like to think that, after painting&nbsp;
this entire universe of shifting identities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.960 --> 00:09:59.120
Raphael decided to leave, at the&nbsp;
exact center of the composition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:04.640
a key to decipher it in the&nbsp;
most subtle way possible.

00:10:04.640 --> 00:10:08.880
I would love for you to leave me in the&nbsp;
comments your opinion on this interpretation.

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:11.600
I will be reading your answers in the comments.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:18.000
As always, thank you for watching the video until&nbsp;
the end, and I will see you in the next video.

